    <h2>The Gutter Is the Cheap Part. The Wood Behind It Isn't.</h2>

    <p>A gutter doesn't attach to the roof — it attaches to the fascia, the flat board running along the eave, which in turn is nailed to the tails of the rafters. That board is the whole foundation of the install, and on the Shoals' older housing it's frequently the problem. When a gutter overflows for years, the water runs down the back of the trough and sits against the fascia, and painted wood that stays wet eventually goes punky and soft. Hang a shiny new seamless gutter on that board and the screws have nothing solid to bite; the gutter holds for a rainy season, then starts to sag and pull the rotten fascia off the house with it. The homeowner sees a failed gutter. What actually failed is the carpentry nobody quoted. This is the piece most Shoals estimates skip entirely, because looking at the wood means getting up on a ladder before the sale instead of after.</p>

    <h2>Three Housing Ages, Three Different Gutter Jobs</h2>

    <p>The Shoals isn't one gutter market — the age of the house sets the job. In <a href="/locations/sheffield/">Sheffield</a>, the oldest housing stock in the region, this is repair-first work: the median home is well past its sixtieth year, a good share predate 1940, and by now the fascia on a lot of these homes has taken decades of overflow. A real gutter job in Sheffield routinely starts with tearing off the old system, replacing runs of soft fascia and the odd rafter tail, and only then hanging new aluminum — the gutter is maybe half the labor. In <a href="/locations/florence/">Florence</a>, the historic districts along Wood Avenue and Walnut Street add a second wrinkle: steep, cut-up rooflines with multiple facets, deep eaves, and homeowners who care how the profile looks from the street, so the job is part carpentry, part matching a gutter that suits a century-old house. Cross east to the newer subdivisions in <a href="/locations/muscle-shoals/">Muscle Shoals</a> and the fascia is usually still sound — there the conversation isn't repair, it's capacity: builder-grade 5-inch gutters and small downspouts that can't move what a hard North Alabama storm drops, overflowing at the front lip. Even quiet Tuscumbia, with its own stretch of old homes, follows the same rule — the older the house, the more the job is about the board, not the aluminum.</p>

    <h2>How the Cheap Bid Turns Into a Callback</h2>

    <p>Picture the low quote on an old Florence cottage. A crew shows up, tears the sagging gutter off, and screws a fresh seamless run straight back onto the same fifty-year-old fascia without a second look. It's tight and straight the day they leave. Then the first heavy spring rain loads the gutter, the screws work in the soft wood, and by summer the run is pitched wrong, pooling water, and pulling visibly away from the eave — sometimes taking a strip of fascia with it. Now the homeowner is paying twice: once for the gutter that failed and again for the fascia repair that should have been in the first quote. The practitioner tell that separates a real installer from a hanger is simple — they get on a ladder and press the fascia before they price the job, because on the Shoals' old homes the wood decides everything and the person who checks it is the only one who can give an honest number.</p>

    <h2 id="pricing-summary">What Gutter Work Costs in the Shoals</h2>

    <p>Real ranges, installed:</p>
    <ul class="trades-list-block">
      <li><strong>5-inch seamless aluminum</strong> — about $7 to $10 per linear foot.</li>
      <li><strong>6-inch seamless aluminum</strong> — about $10 to $14 per linear foot, the right call on a home that overflows.</li>
      <li><strong>Fascia repair or replacement</strong> — priced separately once the old gutter is off; on an old Sheffield or Florence home this is the number that moves the quote most.</li>
      <li><strong>Gutter guards</strong> — roughly $8 to $15 per linear foot.</li>
      <li><strong>Downspout extensions</strong> — added cost, and worth it to carry water clear of the wall.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The old-house numbers swing on one thing: how much fascia has to come off and get rebuilt before new gutter goes up. That's why an honest installer wants the old system off and a look at the wood before committing to a price — anyone quoting a firm all-in on a sixty-year-old home sight-unseen is guessing at the part that matters.</p>

    <h2>When the Wood Is Telling You It's Time</h2>

    <p>A gutter pulling away from the eave, or one section visibly lower than the rest, is usually the fascia letting go, not a loose spike — it's a today problem before the run comes down. Paint peeling or blistering on the fascia board behind the gutter means water is getting behind the trough and sitting on the wood, which is how the rot starts. And gutter spikes or screws that keep backing out no matter how often you tap them in are telling you the wood has nothing left to grip. On the region's older homes, none of those heal on their own; they just get more expensive the longer the board stays wet.</p>

    <h2>Sell the Fascia Fix, Not the Foot of Gutter</h2>

    <p>Here's the opening most Shoals gutter installers are leaving wide open. The homeowner in an old Sheffield house doesn't search for aluminum — they search "gutter repair Sheffield AL" after the last set sagged, and the one in a new Muscle Shoals subdivision searches "gutter installation Muscle Shoals" because theirs overflow every storm. Two different customers, two different jobs, and almost nobody in the region has written a page that names either problem, so the searches leak to whoever ranks — often an out-of-town company that treats the Shoals as one line on a service-area map. A site that explains the real Shoals gutter reality — that on old homes the job is fascia carpentry first, and on new ones it's a sizing fix — wins the homeowner before the ladder ever comes off the truck, and the <a href="/locations/the-shoals/">Shoals contractor overview</a> shows how uncontested those town-level searches still are. The same gap sits open for the exterior trades gutters travel with, like <a href="/locations/the-shoals/roofer/">roofing</a> and <a href="/locations/the-shoals/painter/">painting</a>. <a href="/">Sites On Call</a> builds a real page — designed, not dropped into a template — the way the contractors who win with a <a href="/articles/contractor-website-design/">website built to be found</a> already do it. If the gutter calls are going to out-of-town outfits, let's fix that.</p>

